. * ============================
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4
. * ============================
. 
. * H4: Having a daughter increases support for gender equality (interaction ef
> fect for males)
. 
. * To empirically test this hypothesis, a linear Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) 
> regression model with interaction terms is employed. The dependent variable i
> s an index of gender-egalitarian attitudes (gender_eq_index), constructed by 
> standardizing and averaging responses to survey items related to gender equal
> ity.
. 
. * The key independent variables include a binary indicator (d_daughter) repre
> senting whether respondents have at least one daughter (1) or none (0), and g
> ender of the respondent (sex). An interaction term between these two variable
> s (d_daughter##sex) captures whether the effect of having a daughter on gende
> r equality attitudes is significantly stronger for male respondents compared 
> to female respondents. The regression controls for various demographic and so
> cioeconomic variables, including age, number of children, household size, yea
> rs of education, labor income, migration background, region, survey year, and
>  family status. Standard errors are clustered at the household level to accou
> nt for within-household correlations.
. 
. * The coefficient estimate for the interaction term (d_daughter#sex) specific
> ally tests whether having daughters has a significantly different (and strong
> er) effect on male respondents' attitudes towards gender equality compared to
>  female respondents.
. 
. reg gender_eq_index d_daughter##male age num_kids hhsize pgbilzeit pglabgro i
> .migback eastgermany##y2018 i.pgfamstd , cluster(hid)

